// The idle-worker park and its wake (#1902). Sibling of `worker.bit`, so the
// same module — this is not a new boundary, only a new file, because `worker.bit`
// is at 767 of the 800-line limit.
//
// WHY THE FILE SORTS WHERE IT DOES. Module files concatenate in bytewise
// filename order and the inliner only inlines a callee it has already lowered
// (#1511). `schedWakeIdle` is called from `task.bit` and `worker.bit`, so the
// name has to land before both: sched < scratch < sleep < task < worker. A name
// like `idle.bit` would have read better and sorted before `sched.bit`, losing
// the inlining of everything this file calls.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What was wrong
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// An idle worker slept, but never deeper than `parkMaxBackoffNs` — 1ms. So it
// woke 1000 times a second forever, and each wake ran a find-work pass that
// steals across EVERY other worker. N threads x 1000/s x an O(N) scan, paid
// whether or not the program was doing anything. Measured on an 18-core box, a
// program whose whole body is `sleep(3s)`:
//
//     BIT_WORKERS=1    0.01s CPU        BIT_WORKERS=8    0.20s
//     BIT_WORKERS=2    0.03s            BIT_WORKERS=18   0.84s
//
// 84x the CPU to do nothing. That measurement is why #1900 shipped with a
// default of one worker, and a default of one worker in a language with `spawn`
// is the bug the epic exists to fix.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A park word PER WORKER, and why one shared word does not work
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Each worker owns `wkPark`, an i32 in its own block. 1 means "armed to park, or
// parked"; 0 means "awake, or claimed by a waker". A worker ARMS BEFORE it looks
// for work, and a producer that publishes work scans for an armed worker, claims
// exactly one with a compare-and-exchange, and wakes that one.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION USED ONE SHARED SEQUENCE WORD FOR THE POOL and it was a
// spin, not a park. The mechanism is worth stating because the shared word looks
// obviously cheaper: with one word, EVERY wake invalidates EVERY park. A worker
// hands `parkWait` the sequence value it read, so any bump makes all N-2 sleepers
// return EAGAIN, run a find-work pass, and re-arm — at the rate of whatever is
// bumping. Cost is O(N) per event by construction, and the events are not rare:
// a compiler doing thousands of file reads a second unparks its task on every
// one.
//
// Measured with the CPU-count default, and it got worse each time I narrowed the
// wake sites, because narrowing does not change the shape:
//
//     load 68.8 then 128 on an 18-core box, against a target of 25
//     `bit` TYPECHECK invocations blowing a 300s deadline (no program even runs)
//     h3fetch TIMED OUT
//     worker threads in state R at ~12% CPU each — spinning, not sleeping
//
// Strictly worse than the 1kHz polling this file exists to remove. A per-worker
// word has no such coupling: a wake disturbs one worker, and the others sleep
// through it. The producer pays a bounded scan of at most `nworkers` words
// instead, which is a few cache lines and no syscall.
//
// THE ARM ORDER IS THE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT, and it is the only subtle part.
// The worker arms BEFORE its find-work pass; the producer publishes the work
// BEFORE it scans. Take the two interleavings:
//
//   - producer published first -> the worker's own find-work pass sees the work.
//     The producer may also claim and wake it; that wake lands on a worker that
//     is about to disarm and run, and a `FUTEX_WAKE` with no waiter is a no-op.
//   - worker armed first, published after -> the scan sees `wkPark == 1`, claims
//     it, and wakes. If the worker has not reached `parkWait` yet, the word is
//     already 0, so `parkWait`'s own compare returns immediately rather than
//     sleeping. That compare IS the double-check; there is no separate re-poll.
//
// Arming AFTER find-work instead reopens the window this closes: a producer that
// publishes between the failed find-work and the arm sees no armed worker, and
// the worker then sleeps through work that is already queued.
//
// `scIdleCount` survives as an OPTIMISATION AND ONLY THAT: it lets a producer
// skip the scan entirely when nobody is parked. A stale read either way is
// harmless — a stale zero skips a scan that would have found nothing settled
// anyway (the armed worker's own find-work pass is still ahead of it), a stale
// non-zero costs one bounded scan.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Worker 0 does not park, and that is the safety property
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Only workers 1..N-1 park. Worker 0 keeps exactly today's loop: a 1ms backoff
// ramp, a find-work pass, a `pollDrain`. Two things fall out of that, and both
// are the reason this is the shape that landed rather than a general park:
//
//   - NO MISSED WAKE CAN HANG A PROGRAM. Worker 0 is always looking, so the
//     worst a wake this file forgets to issue can do is leave a task waiting up
//     to `idleParkMaxNs` for a peer that would have run it in parallel. Latency,
//     not a deadlock. For a change whose entire risk is the lost wakeup, having
//     the failure mode be "slower" instead of "wedged" is worth one thread.
//
//   - THE NETPOLLER AND THE TIMER QUEUE NEED NO NEW MACHINERY. Both are driven
//     off the idle path — `pollDrain` from the loop, `schedTimerExpire` from
//     `schedFindWorkAt` — so a design where every worker parks has to first
//     arrange for somebody to still notice a ready socket or a due deadline.
//     That means blocking one worker IN `epoll_wait` with a self-pipe or eventfd
//     for producers to poke, which is a second mechanism with a second set of
//     races. Leaving worker 0 awake buys the same result for nothing.
//
// The idle cost therefore stops scaling with the worker count without becoming
// zero: it is one thread's worth at any N, which is what the acceptance measures.
// Parking worker 0 too is real work and is deliberately not attempted here.

// `bit_rt_port_park_wake` (`runtime/park/<os>`, pinned by ed51ca2). Declared
// `extern` rather than imported because this module may import neither park
// provider — the same rule, and the same resolution, as `sched.bit`'s
// `bit_rt_time_mono_ns` ten files over: a `@nosplit` body may call an `extern`
// function but not a non-`@nosplit` Bit one (E0075, #1640), and `parkWake` is
// not `@nosplit` because it makes a syscall. E0078 validates the name against
// `libbitrt.a` at this declaration, so a rename that forgets this line is a
// compile error rather than a link one.
extern fn bit_rt_port_park_wake(p: *i32, all: bool): i64
extern fn bit_rt_port_park_wait(p: *i32, expected: i32, timeoutNs: i64): i64

// The longest a parked worker sleeps without being woken. A pure safety net —
// see the header: worker 0 never parks, so nothing depends on this firing.
//
// 100ms rather than the 1ms the old ceiling used: at 1ms the park would still be
// 1000 wakes a second per worker and would fix nothing, which is the trap in
// "keep the existing ceiling as the bound". At 100ms, 17 parked workers cost 170
// wakes a second between them against 17,000 before. It is not longer still
// because a bound that never plausibly fires stops being a net anyone tests.
export const idleParkMaxNs: int = 100000000

// How many extra find-work passes an idle worker makes in USERSPACE before it
// goes to the kernel (#3260). Consumed as ONE burst, and re-armed only by a turn
// that actually found work — see `runtime/root/<os>`'s `workerBody`, which owns
// the burst because the find-work seam it drives is per-platform.
//
// WHY A SPIN AT ALL. Every contended `std/sync` lock is `<- m.token` on a
// capacity-1 channel, so at any instant exactly ONE task is runnable and each
// handoff republishes it microseconds later. Parking after a single failed pass
// makes every one of those handoffs an OS round trip — `__ulock_wake` on the
// releasing side, `__ulock_wait` to re-park — and the cost per acquisition then
// grows with the worker count instead of staying flat: measured at 15.6 ns for
// one worker, 406 ns for four and 1172 ns for eight, with 88% of thread time in
// park/wake leaves (#2321). A worker that keeps looking for a few microseconds
// picks the republished task up with no syscall at all.
//
// WHY IT IS RE-ARMED ONLY BY FOUND WORK, which is the part that keeps #1902's
// result: if a park re-armed it, an idle pool would burn a burst per wake
// forever and the measurement that pinned the worker default at 1 (a program
// whose whole body is `sleep(3s)` costing 0.84s of CPU at 18 workers) would come
// straight back. Re-arming on found work makes the burst proportional to the
// work the worker is actually doing — a worker with nothing left to do pays it
// exactly once and then sleeps.
//
// WHY 32. A find-work pass steals across every peer's deque, so a spinning
// worker touches the head/tail lines of the worker trying to make progress;
// past a point the spin costs the holder more than it saves the spinner. 32
// passes is a few microseconds here — short enough to stay noise against
// `idleParkMaxNs` when nothing is coming.
//
// IT NO LONGER COVERS A LOCK HANDOFF, and that used to be this constant's
// whole reason (#3264). A handoff now lands in the waker's own `runnext`
// slot (`queue.bit`), which `dqSteal` cannot see and which the SAME thread
// drains on its own very next find-work pass with no wake published at all
// — there is nothing left for a spinning peer to catch. Measured at
// `9ac1aeb8` (#3317): rebuilding with `idleSpinTurns = 0` moved the
// contended single-Mutex workload from 2304 to 2301 instructions per
// acquisition, and the 8-lock/64-thread case from 2322 to 2319, `sys` at
// 0.00 throughout — the spin is inert on the workload this comment used to
// cite.
//
// WHAT IT STILL REACHES: a netpoller or timer wake found off a task's own
// stack has `schedCurrentWorker() == 0`, so `schedUnpark` takes its OTHER
// branch — `gqPush` onto the global queue, not the slot — and that IS
// visible to a spin, through `schedWorkPending`'s `gqNonEmpty` probe and
// `schedFindWork`'s `gqPop`.
//
// THAT CASE IS NOW MEASURED (#3348), AND THE SPIN LOSES ON IT — narrowly, and
// on every other workload tried. The value stays at 32 here because this ticket
// was scoped as the measurement, not the change; see the numbers before moving
// it, and cover the global-queue wake sources this did not (a spawn burst, a
// cross-worker `chan` handoff) first.
//
// Two workloads, neither a lock handoff, each proven by per-worker counters
// inside the runtime to drive the wake source it claims rather than
// degenerating into the other: a 4-connection loopback TCP ping-pong reports
// 159,8xx netpoller wakes of 160,000 blocking reads and 0 timer wakes, and 8
// green threads sleeping in a loop report 160,000 timer wakes and 0 netpoller
// wakes. Metric is instructions retired per wake, `idleSpinTurns` swept
// 0,1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512; the figures below are 32 against 0, 12
// interleaved rounds each, permutation test on the medians:
//
//   netpoller, 4 workers    +4.2%   p=0.008   context switches -2.9%
//   netpoller, default      +0.25%  p=0.14    context switches -3.7%
//   timer 20us, 4 workers   +0.54%  p=0.12
//   timer 200us, 4 workers  +0.47%  p=0.009
//
// So the spin does what it says — parks fall monotonically with the budget, by
// 3-8% — and pays more for them than they are worth. Wall clock is unchanged
// (0.665s either way on the netpoller workload), so it is not buying latency
// back either. Above 32 it is a plain loss everywhere: +4.1% at 64, and the
// block sweep put 512 at +30% on the netpoller workload and +95% on the timer.
//
// WHY, from the counters: a 32-pass burst runs to EXHAUSTION about 95% of the
// time (31.4 passes of 32 on average), and the pass on which a hit lands is
// distributed almost uniformly across the budget rather than clustering early —
// so there is no knee to tune to. It finds work in 4% of bursts on the
// netpoller workload and 0-3 times per 15,000 parks at a 200us timer cadence.
//
// MEASURE IT INTERLEAVED, NOT IN BLOCKS, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT WILL BITE
// THE NEXT PERSON. Two block-design sweeps — seven consecutive runs of one
// budget, then seven of the next — each reported a confident 4% BENEFIT at a
// budget of 4, with the same curve shape both times, which is exactly what a
// real effect looks like. It was an artifact of the machine drifting through
// the value order: `spin = 0` runs first, at the highest load of any low-budget
// arm in both sweeps (3.75 and 5.41 against 2.24-3.98 for the rest). A
// SCHEDULER's instruction count is itself load-dependent — contention produces
// more failed find-work passes and more park/wake cycles — so "instructions
// retired are load-insensitive" is true of the COUNTER and false of what the
// counter counts here. Round-robin the budgets so every arm sees the same
// conditions and the 4% benefit inverts into the 4.2% loss above; two
// interleaved runs at very different loads agree with each other and disagree
// with both block runs.
//
// COST PER PASS, for anyone re-deriving this rather than trusting it: one pass
// is `123 + 50*(nworkers - 1)` instructions — 19 in this loop plus
// `schedWorkPending`'s own path — so 273 at four workers and 8,736 for a full
// 32-pass burst. A structurally identical `cc -O2` C loop probing the same six
// queues is 78. That 3.5x is near-constant in the worker count, and is NOT the
// 10x the older "41 instructions against clang's 4" figure implies; this loop
// is dominated by memory probes, which both compilers lower alike.
export const idleSpinTurns: int = 32

// Upper bound on the wake scan, so the loop's termination is provable from its
// own body (Power of 10) rather than from trusting `scNWorkers`. Must not be
// BELOW the consumers' own ceiling — `runtime/root/<os>`'s `bootMaxWorkers`,
// which is 32 and is what actually clamps the count at boot — or a worker past
// this index would arm and never be claimed. Kept as a separate constant because
// those live in a module this one may not import.
export const schedMaxWorkers: int = 32

// This worker's park word as the i32 the futex compares.
//
// The arithmetic is written out rather than calling `sched.bit`'s `lockOf`, which
// does exactly this. Not an oversight: the consumers are `runtime/root/<os>`'s
// boot, which emits `--freestanding` (SPEC §11.9), and THE INLINER SPLICES
// SINGLE-BLOCK CALLEES ACROSS THAT EDGE — so routing through `lockOf` lands an
// unpinned `m6$lockOf` reference inside boot's object that no sibling defines and
// E0078 rejects at link. Pinning the callee does not help, because the inline
// happens first. Mirror any change to `lockOf` here.
@nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_park_addr") fn workerParkAddr(w: *i64): *i32 {
  let z: *i32 = nil
  return z + int(w + wkPark) / 4
}

// Arm this worker to park. MUST be called before its find-work pass, not after —
// see the header's interleaving argument.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_idle_arm") fn schedIdleArm(w: *i64) {
  atomicStore(workerParkAddr(w), 1)
}

// Found work instead. Clear the arm so a producer does not spend its one wake on
// a worker that is already running.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_idle_disarm") fn schedIdleDisarm(w: *i64) {
  atomicStore(workerParkAddr(w), 0)
}

// Park until claimed, or until `idleParkMaxNs` elapses.
//
// `parkWait` compares the word against 1 under its own lock: a producer that
// claimed this worker between the arm and here has already stored 0, so the wait
// returns immediately rather than sleeping through a wake. The caller owns the
// `gcBlockingBegin`/`gcBlockingEnd` bracket (ABI.md §5) — a parked worker cannot
// reach a safepoint, so the collector must see it as blocking or every rendezvous
// waits it out.
//
// The word is cleared unconditionally on the way out, whichever way the wait
// ended: woken, timed out, or returned immediately.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_idle_park") fn schedIdlePark(s: *i64, w: *i64) {
  atomicAdd(s + scIdleCount, 1)
  let _ = bit_rt_port_park_wait(workerParkAddr(w), 1, idleParkMaxNs)
  atomicStore(workerParkAddr(w), 0)
  atomicSub(s + scIdleCount, 1)
}

// Work is now reachable by another worker: wake exactly one parked worker.
//
// Call sites are the transitions that PUBLISH work others can reach — a spill to
// the global queue, a spawn, an unpark, a poller unpark. An ordinary yield back
// onto the running worker's own ring is not one of them, and waking for it was
// half of what made the shared-word version spin. The test for a new call site
// is not "did something become runnable" but "can a DIFFERENT worker now find
// work it could not find a moment ago".
//
// The scan is bounded by the worker count, itself clamped to 32 at boot, so the
// loop's bound is provable from this body (Power of 10). Claiming with a
// compare-and-exchange is what keeps two producers from spending two wakes on the
// same worker.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_wake_idle") fn schedWakeIdle(s: *i64) {
  if (atomicLoad(s + scIdleCount) <= 0) {
    return
  }
  let n = atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n && i < schedMaxWorkers) {
    let p = workerParkAddr(schedWorkerAt(s, i))
    if (atomicCmpxchg(p, 1, 0)) {
      let _ = bit_rt_port_park_wake(p, false)
      return
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// Shutdown's wake, and the one caller that genuinely wants the whole pool: every
// worker must observe `scStopping` and leave, so waking one would exit one worker
// per `idleParkMaxNs` and turn teardown into a staircase.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_port_sched_wake_idle_all") fn schedWakeIdleAll(s: *i64) {
  let n = atomicLoad(s + scNWorkers)
  let i = 0
  while (i < n && i < schedMaxWorkers) {
    let p = workerParkAddr(schedWorkerAt(s, i))
    atomicStore(p, 0)
    let _ = bit_rt_port_park_wake(p, true)
    i = i + 1
  }
}
